  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "In the first sense **smell** links the thing to a description, like **look** or **sound**. In the second sense it has an object and means *notice*; here it usually goes with **can** or **could** and not in continuous tenses. In the third sense, where you smell something on purpose, continuous forms are normal: *He was smelling the flowers*. The past form **smelled** is usual in American English; **smelt** is also common in British English.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "This soup smells well.",
      "correct": "This soup smells good.",
      "note": "When **smell** describes a smell, it is followed by an adjective, not an adverb."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "I am smelling gas.",
      "correct": "I can smell gas.",
      "note": "When **smell** means *notice a smell*, use **can** or **could**, not a continuous form. A continuous form is used only for smelling something on purpose."
    }
  ],
